Application: Predicted variables:
Opinion distribution alignment for demographic groups: Predicting the distributions of human answers to 100 questions from the OpinionQA and GlobalOpinionQA dataset. Questions spanning topics such as:
- science, politics, and personal relationships; 
- perspectives on global issues across various countries;
- Likelihood of reading a specific book

HEXACO-100 personality inventory (Honesty-Humility, Emotionality, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Conscientiousness, and Openness to Experience)  without the four interstitial altruism items. Responses were captured on a Likert-type scale from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree;
Twenty items from the Balanced Inventory of Desirable Responding (BIDR; Paulhus 1991) were used to measure IM. Responses were captured on a Likert-type scale from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree;
Manipulation Check Questions:
"I felt motivated to be one of the top scorers on the assessment”, (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree);
"The position for which I was applying was:", (Marketing Manager, Graduate Accounts Clerk, Accountant, Social Worker);
"As a final part of this study, we want to ask about your use of Generative AI (Gen AI) in this study. Most people use these technologies through online platforms such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Bing Chat, or Claude. To what extent (if any) did you use Gen AI to complete the assessment in the study you just completed?", (1 = Not at all: I did not use Gen AI at all for generating my responses; 2 = Rarely: I used Gen AI for generating a few parts of my responses; 3 = Occasionally: I used Gen AI for generating about half of my responses; 4 = Frequently: I used Gen AI for generating most of my responses; 5 = Always: I used Gen AI for generating all of my responses);

Training Dataset Size:
Three different Fine-tuning methods and therefore datasets were tested: 

(1) Random: 80% of fine-tuning data used for training, with unseen topic-country pairs as the evaluation set; 
(2) Country-based: 20% of countries withheld (n = 11 for WVS, n = 8 for PEW); 
(3) Topic-based: 20% of topics withheld (n = 4 for WVS, n = 2 for PEW). The resulting training and evaluation sample counts: WVS -> training samples range from 81,200-82,600 with 202-216 evaluation pairs depending on strategy; PEW -> training samples range from 23,400-24,900 with 63-78 evaluation pairs.

Application: Predicted variables:
Distributions of survey response ; Consistency metrics of survey responses; Diversity of survey responses across demographic subgroups

